docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:5:A real GPT-tier external pass ran through the agent's codex CLI in EVERY round (5/5 ok — verdicts: MINOR ISSUES ×3, SERIOUS ISSUES ×1 [round 3, findings folded/dispositioned], MINOR ISSUES final with "no serious blocker found"). gemini-cli (gemini-2.5-pro) ran ok in rounds 1 and 3 (MINOR ISSUES / SERIOUS ISSUES, findings folded) and timed out (degraded) in rounds 2, 4, 5 — each timeout recorded per-round; the spec-level flag is the clean `codex-cli:gpt-5.5` because genuine external passes succeeded in every round. The Anthropic clean-door read was refused by the deployed module (`untrusted-framework` — the installed dist predates the claude-code trusted-family entry) and is recorded honestly as not-run; it would not have counted as cross-family either way.
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:13:On July 10 one of the operator's machines crashed for half an hour. Messages for its conversations arrived at the other machine, whose routing brain correctly said "not mine — queue it." Six milliseconds later, older code ignored that ruling and started local copies anyway. When the crashed machine came back, there were two live copies of each conversation, and every safety rule that normally prevents killing a session — each one correct on its own — combined to make the duplicates unkillable. A human had to clean it up. It was the second time in a week.
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:15:This spec fixes that three ways. First, prevention: one checkpoint that every session-creating code path must pass, which makes the routing verdict binding — a machine that doesn't own a conversation can never start a bootleg copy of it; if the owner is temporarily dark, the user gets held-then-honest-notice behavior with a hard 10-minute silence ceiling, never silence and never a lie about whether the message was saved. Second, healing: a reconciler on the lead machine that spots duplicates, determines the rightful owner from real evidence (never "who got the last message" — during this bug the wrong copy gets the last message), repairs the ownership records everywhere, verifies the other machine actually saw the repair, and lets the existing safe cleanup close the spare — after re-checking at closing time that the survivor is genuinely alive, and after handing over any open promises so nothing the agent committed to gets silently dropped. Third, a new pattern: the two genuinely fuzzy judgment calls in this flow (how long to wait on a wobbly machine; which duplicate survives on messy evidence) can be delegated to an LLM arbiter — but only inside deterministic rails, with a conservative default, mechanical corroboration for anything irreversible, full decision logging, outcome grading against reality, and a rule that the arbiter only gets authority after shadow data proves it beats the simple static rule.
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:17:The main tradeoffs, stated honestly: the design uses instar's existing coordination machinery rather than adding an external consensus system, which means ownership is NOT perfectly guaranteed during network partitions or record-store errors — divergence in those windows is bounded, loud, and self-converging rather than impossible. And when ownership records are unreadable, the system deliberately fails toward answering the user (a bounded, breaker-limited duplicate is better than silence).
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:21:**Originally**, the auto-heal converged the ownership record and assumed the existing cleanup would then close the spare copy. Review proved that assumption wrong against the incident itself: the cleanup re-checks every keep-guard fresh, so the spare's open commitment would STILL have made it immortal — the exact July-10 outcome. **Converged:** a narrow, provenance-fenced carve-out for exactly the two topic-bookkeeping guards, valid only with a terminate-time proof that the surviving copy is live, plus an acknowledged cross-machine handoff of open commitments (redesigned twice: the first "re-bind" referenced a session field that doesn't exist on commitment records; the final design mints a successor on the owner machine and terminally supersedes the origin record only on acknowledgment, behind its own independently-reversible rollout flag — until that flag flips, commitment-shielded duplicates escalate to the operator instead of auto-closing).
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:29:**Originally**, the spec implied stronger consistency than the substrate provides. **Converged:** an explicit consistency contract (§3.0) states the safety and liveness guarantees AND the non-goals — no linearizable ownership, no guaranteed single owner under partition — each mapped to a bounded/loud/converge-back mechanism, with a partition/delayed-replay test matrix.
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:37:| 3 | adversarial, integration, lessons-aware + codex + gemini; sec/scale/dc effectively clean | 5 (deduped) | v4: consistency contract; topic-moved fence made satisfiable; episode-open = spawn timestamp + work-gate probe restriction; custody sub-flag (Increment 2b); gitignore migration parity; retention 14d |
docs/specs/reports/ownership-gated-spawn-and-judgment-within-floors-convergence.md:38:| 4 | adversarial (1) + codex minor-only (gemini timeout); sec/scale/int clean | 1 | v5: terminate-time re-probe universal (every reconciled/moved termination); §0 exemption row widened; consistency minors |
