docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:74:a reason to record *nothing*. It is not a licence to record *indiscriminately*, and reading it that
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:90:| `observedOptionsDigest` | **An equality check over a NORMALIZED option model, not over raw screen text.** See §2.5 — digesting raw text would measure line wrapping, ANSI codes, and redraws, so it would fire constantly on presentation and prove nothing about drift. Keyed (HMAC-SHA-256, `digestKeyId`) because option labels are low-entropy and a bare hash of "1. Yes / 2. No" falls to a dictionary in seconds. A record carrying only the digest is NOT replayable and is `degraded` by definition. |
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:134:| `timestamp` + source | a record whose clock is unattributable correlates with nothing |
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:135:| chaining | each record carries the prior record's hash. **On writer start the chain is re-anchored from the file tail**, and a re-anchor is itself a recorded event — instar restarts on every auto-update, so a verifier that treated a boot boundary as tampering would be worse than no chain. |
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:136:| write failure | appends MUST NOT throw into the decision path. `sequence` is assigned only on success, so a gap always means deletion and never a dropped write. **Every failed append is itself durably recorded** — outside this log, carrying component, time, failure reason, and the decision class attempted. That record is the evidence that a decision happened unrecorded; without it the FIRST failure is already an unaccountable decision by §1's own definition, and a threshold of N would silently permit N−1 of them. **The failure sink can itself fail** — disk-full and permission errors hit both logs — so the ladder terminates in-process: durable sink, else the server log, else an in-memory counter surfaced on the component's health surface. The last rung survives nothing, and saying so is the point: there is no durable guarantee left at that depth, and a spec claiming one would be inventing it. The threshold governs only when the OPERATOR is notified, not when the failure is recorded. Whether the component keeps deciding is out of scope (§5b); whether it does so invisibly is not. |
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:171:that actor can recompute every subsequent link. Detecting *that* requires an anchor the writer cannot
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:175:the server log, which has a different lifecycle and rotation. A component may use a stronger anchor.
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:176:Without an anchor the honest claim is only "mid-chain edits are detectable", and a spec claiming more
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:200:**Honest limit:** normalization means a change invisible to this model is invisible to the digest. A
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:345:(ACT-1312) — there is nothing to grade until the record carries matched spans and an observed digest.
docs/specs/decision-replayability-standard.md:382:| failure behaviour | fail CLOSED. Unresolvable authorization returns nothing and never degrades to serving records. |
docs/specs/real-channel-absence-collect.md:39:- **Failed read → BLOCKED.** Slack `ok === false` (auth revoked / not_in_channel) throws `AbsenceUnverifiableError` — an absence proof must never green over a read that returned nothing because it FAILED. (Symmetric with `send`'s existing `!ts` guard.)
docs/specs/real-channel-absence-collect.md:68:- **D4 — Truncated/paginated/failed read → BLOCKED (AbsenceUnverifiableError), never PASS.** Completeness is the whole value of an absence proof; an unprovable read must block, not green. The Telegram check is marker-bounded (a full page is truncation only when its oldest entry is still after the marker — so a reused demo topic isn't wrongly blocked); Slack is `oldest`-bounded natively. *Cheap-to-change-after:* the guard is a per-poll page-bound check; this is test-harness infra behind no published interface and ships nothing dark/irreversible.
