docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:208:| When it runs — EXACT ORDER (round-8 finding 1) | `pnpm build` runs: (1) clean `dist/`, (2) `tsc` compile, (3) **then** the generator writes `dist/data/*`, (4) then verification/packaging. The generator MUST run AFTER clean and compile — a clean step running later would delete `dist/data`, and the artifact would be silently missing from the package: this spec's own defect, reintroduced through build ordering. Asserted by a test that runs the REAL build and checks the artifact survives it, never by reading the script. |
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:210:| Release check — wired into the path publish ALREADY takes (round-9 finding 1) | The verifier runs as a step of the existing **`prepublishOnly`** chain (today: `npm run build && npm run check:upgrade-guide && npm run check:contract-evidence`), which `npm publish` invokes itself. Verified: there is no `prepack` script, and `prepublishOnly` already rebuilds — so a verifier there inspects the exact tree publish then packs. A separate `npm pack` in a CI step would verify a DIFFERENT pack invocation and prove nothing about the published artifact, which is precisely the trusting-a-proxy mistake this spec exists to remove. `scripts/verify-standards-registry-asset.mjs` asserts **authored document == `dist/data/standards-registry.md` == the meta** (three-way, by sha256) over that tree, and additionally produces a real `npm pack --json --pack-destination <tmp>` tarball in CI to confirm the include actually carries the files. |
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:211:| Why a real pack | `npm pack --dry-run` reports the file LIST and cannot hand you bytes. An inclusion-only check would trust a listing instead of the artifact — this spec's own sin. Dry-run is the cheap per-PR inclusion signal only, never the guarantee. |
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:212:| Lifecycle invariant (round-10 finding 2) | The design relies on nothing mutating `dist/` AFTER verification. Today that holds — no `prepack`, no `prepare`. Because "it holds today" is exactly the assumption that produced this spec, it becomes an ASSERTED invariant rather than a hope: a repo-invariants check fails if a `prepack`/`prepare`/`postbuild` script is introduced without the artifact verifier re-running after it. A future maintainer adding one gets a red check explaining why, not a silently missing asset. |
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:271:Should someone later add a TS-direct command, `../data` would resolve under `src/data` where nothing
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:278:cleans `dist`).
docs/specs/standards-registry-snapshot-refresh.md:299:    it maintains nothing once the readers use the resolver.
