docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:66:- Permissions are **stored and injected into session context** as a `[SYSTEM-ENFORCED PERMISSIONS]` block (`UserContextBuilder`) — i.e. the LLM *sees* them as hints, but **nothing gates on them in code**. The "enforcement" today is the model reading a context block and choosing to honor it. That is willpower, not structure.
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:142:- `workspaceMode: 'shared'`, `autoJoinChannels: false` → she joins nothing on her own; a human invites her per channel.
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:177:- **Fail mode:** fail to **silence** (if the gate errors, say nothing). This is the opposite of the floor gate, which fails closed-to-deny.
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:190:**Yes, supported — each agent is its own Slack app / bot user** (its own `botToken`/`appToken`). They coexist as distinct members; humans `@mention` the one they want (`@instar-research` vs `@instar-ops`). This is cleaner than a shared multiplexed bot:
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:209:- **Relayed requests:** if registered user A asks on behalf of unregistered B, the **principal is A** — A's authority applies and A is accountable. Overheard mentions of B confer nothing.
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:377:| `outsider-omar` | (unregistered) | not in the workspace registry; nothing he says is actionable |
docs/specs/SLACK-ORG-INTEGRATION-SPEC.md:474:- **Fail directions are deliberate and opposite:** the **floor gate fails closed** (deny on error — never let a money/prod/credential action through on a timeout); the **ambient/should-speak gate fails to silence** (say nothing on error); the **classifier fails to clarify/escalate**, never to silent-allow.
