docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:5:UNSOUND — three independent egress/model mismatches can admit content that Telegram renders as nothing. Three additional load-bearing claims are false of the code or Telegram’s live grammar. Previously stated-open defects are not counted again.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:15:   [invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:125) classifies `sendRichMessage` and `sendRichMessageDraft` as accepting alternative fields `text` and `rich_message`. They do not: the live API requires only `rich_message` for both methods ([sendRichMessage](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#sendrichmessage), [sendRichMessageDraft](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#sendrichmessagedraft)), and the server reads only `rich_message` ([official implementation](https://github.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/blob/master/telegram-bot-api/Client.cpp#L11607-L11619)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:19:   Concrete failure path: a rich-message request supplies a harmless visible query `text` and carries the actual reader-invisible `rich_message` in a byte-oriented JSON body. The door treats unsupported `text` as decisive, never inspects the body, and sends it; Telegram ignores `text` and consumes `rich_message`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:21:   The pinning test repeats the wrong table while claiming live-documentation verification ([telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:542)), disproving the spec’s assertion that this pin makes silent drift impossible ([spec](/Users/justin/window12-exam/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:329)). This is an intersection of declared residuals, not a recount of either one alone.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:25:   `structuredTextLeaves` loops through every property and recursively descends every object-valued property ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:432)). Telegram instead selects a `type` discriminator and extracts only that variant’s defined fields before returning; for example, URL consumes `text` and `url`, while reference consumes `text` and `name` ([official parser](https://github.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/blob/master/telegram-bot-api/Client.cpp#L11326-L11443)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:27:   Concrete failure path: a valid wrapper has an invisible real label and an extra, server-ignored nested member containing a visible-looking `text` leaf. The door counts the ignored leaf and allows; Telegram discards it and renders only the invisible label.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:29:   This contradicts the source claim that the walker visits “exactly” the literals ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:368)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:35:   The walker classifies `expression` as an ordinary unformatted string ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:432)), then accepts it whenever source characters satisfy the Unicode predicate ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:464)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:47:   [invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:401) says `name` is the displayed reference label and that excluding it wrongly refuses a reference whose only visible content is its name.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:55:   [window12-closeout.md](/Users/justin/window12-exam/docs/specs/reports/window12-closeout.md:40) says the closed world contains 17 methods: four reader-visible plus 13 bodyless.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:57:   The code contains six reader-visible methods ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:125)) and 13 bodyless methods ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:184)), totaling 19. The same closeout later reports 19 ([window12-closeout.md](/Users/justin/window12-exam/docs/specs/reports/window12-closeout.md:207)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:69:- **`answerCallbackQuery`:** Still a stated product judgment rather than a mechanical no-content fact; it remains bodyless ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:176)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:71:- **`editForumTopic`:** Still guarded unconditionally through `name`; empty-name/preserve-current semantics remain unmodeled ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:140)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:85:- **Destination fields:** Excluding live URL/e-mail/phone/anchor/reference-link destinations remains correct because the displayed label is `text`. The claimed `RichTextReference.name` exception is wrong, per Finding 5. `document`/`RichTextIcon` is absent from the live Bot API RichText union, so the “live grammar” comment is also stale there.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:87:- **No-leaf structures:** Still open and unsafe. All no-leaf objects are allowed ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:464)), but the union contains both visible media/dividers and non-rendered anchors. Telegram expressly permits an empty named anchor.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:95:- **Rendering shortcut / CMT-1260:** Still open. HTML/Markdown are locally approximated ([invisible-payload.ts](/Users/justin/window12-exam/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:298)); the stated malformed-tag over-refusal and encoded/delimiter under-refusals remain. Its description is incomplete because LaTeX adds Finding 3.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:103:- **Blinded door and aliased fetch:** Still open. The lint explicitly does not validate method-recovery behavior or follow a fetch reference stored under another name. Its categorical clean output therefore remains broader than its analysis.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass47-verdict.md:107:The directory has no Git metadata, so the stated SHA, cleanliness, and local/remote equality could not be independently verified from this copy.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:106:*Epoch finding (gap-confirmed — drives R4b, where the anchoring decision is now SETTLED, not deferred): the pairing-epoch does NOT exist in the codebase and is not yet designed; it is only named.* Verified: there is `FencedLease.leaseEpoch` (who-is-awake) and a CoherenceJournal `topic-placement` epoch — neither is a per-PAIRING epoch, and neither increments on de-pair. De-pair today (`MachineRegistryEntry`, `types.ts` lines 1740–1754) only flips `status:'revoked'` + sets `revokedAt`/`revokedBy`; it does NOT rotate the machine's static `encryptionPublicKey` (`types.ts` line 1698) and does NOT bump any epoch. A revocation guarantee built on an undesigned counter is a wish, not a mechanism — so the epoch is **promoted from an Open Question to a first-class designed primitive in this round** (this is the security round, before code; designing it here is the mandate). **Lean (R4b): rotate the machine's X25519 `encryptionPublicKey` on de-pair INSTEAD of inventing a new counter.** A rotated recipient key makes EVERY old sealed payload undecryptable, achieving S3 (replay-defeat) and S4 (forward-revocation of sealed blobs) at once, reusing existing identity machinery — leaving only R10's plaintext config-home residual (S4/gap-6) to disclose honestly. The AAD's `pairingEpoch` term then binds to the key-rotation generation. The key-rotation lean is chosen and the anchoring is SETTLED in R4b (OS-keychain-where-available / AES-256-GCM-`SecretStore`-fallback, never a bare JSON file, with a rollback-resistance invariant and a durable cross-reboot read path) — not left as an open question, because PR1 ships a working AAD check against the value.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:124:*Mitigations:* authorizing Mechanism B onto a target is **equivalent to granting that machine a live credential** — so the target MUST clear the SAME trust floor as a Mechanism-A recipient (R9's "established, trust-gated mesh peer"), NOT a lower bar just because "nothing crossed the wire" (specified in R6, R9). Apply S6's host-allowlist + completed-account-email validation to the target-side `driveLogin` EXPLICITLY and BEFORE the account is selectable: a target-supplied `verificationUrl`/`userCode` is untrusted (host-allowlist) even when minted on the target, and the completed account's email is validated against operator expectation before the account becomes usable. State plainly: Mechanism B is safer for *blast-radius* (no transportable credential leaves) but NOT for "is this machine trustworthy enough to hold a real login" — **both mechanisms require the target to be trusted with a live credential.**
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:156:**R4b — De-pair forward-revocation: rotate the recipient X25519 key (the chosen pairing-epoch mechanism).** The "pairing-epoch" named across S3/S4/I2/I4/R12.iii does NOT exist in the codebase (verified: only `FencedLease.leaseEpoch` and a placement epoch exist; de-pair flips `status:'revoked'` and rotates nothing — `types.ts` lines 1740–1754). It is promoted from Open Question to a designed primitive here. **Chosen lean: on de-pair, ROTATE the machine's X25519 `encryptionPublicKey`** (reusing existing identity machinery) — this makes EVERY old sealed payload undecryptable, delivering S3 (replay-defeat) and S4's sealed-blob inertness at once, with no new counter to anchor. The AAD's `pairingEpoch` term binds to the key-rotation generation.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:158:**Anchoring decision — SETTLED in this round (was OQ3; resolved per "design-fork decisions are mine," because PR1 must ship a working `decryptAccountCredential` AAD check against `pairingEpoch`, so the anchor cannot remain an open question):** the rotated key material AND its generation marker are durably anchored in the **OS keychain where available (macOS Keychain / Linux Secret Service), falling back to the AES-256-GCM `SecretStore`** (`SecretStore.ts` lines 5, 7, 93–94 — the same master-key-in-keychain mechanism the secret store already uses), **NEVER a bare plaintext JSON file a hostile local root can revert.** The receiver learns the CURRENT key/generation across reboot by reading this anchor (keychain-first, encrypted-store fallback) at decrypt time — NOT from an in-memory or plaintext-on-disk value. **Load-bearing invariant (NEW):** *a hostile local root that reverts the on-disk epoch/key anchor MUST NOT be able to make an old sealed blob decryptable.* The keychain master key is not revertible by an on-disk file rollback, and the encrypted-store fallback is GCM-authenticated, so reverting the on-disk ciphertext to an older generation does not yield the old private key — the captured-blob replay (S3) fails the AAD/tag check regardless. The build MUST still define: (1) what a "pairing" is and that the rotation is persisted to this anchor on de-pair, (2) that de-pair triggers the rotation (a counter may additionally be bumped, but the X25519 key rotation is the load-bearing mechanism), (3) the durable cross-reboot read path described above.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:160:**Honest limitation:** key-rotation makes the SEALED blob inert; it does NOT touch the framework's plaintext config-home token (S4 layer-2 limitation, gap 6) — that residue is handled by the active wipe verb + short TTL + provider rotation, not by rotation. **Tier-1 test:** a sealed blob fails to decrypt after key-rotation — proven ONLY under the new key/AAD scheme, NOT under the current single-key store. **Anchor-rollback test (extends §8.4 "Key-rotation durability across reboot"):** after de-pair + rotation, revert the on-disk anchor to its pre-rotation state and assert an old captured sealed blob STILL fails to decrypt (proves resistance to anchor-rollback, not merely reboot survival). (Guards S3, S4; gaps 3, 7.)
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:166:**R6a — The cross-machine enroll-drive transport MUST be defined before Mechanism B is claimed Phase-C-ready (gap-confirmed: it does not exist today).** Verified: the only production `LoginDriver` is `FrameworkLoginDriver` (`src/core/FrameworkLoginDriver.ts` line 43), whose `deps.spawn` opens a LOCAL tmux pane in the server's own process and scrapes it (`drive()` lines 107–138). There is NO cross-machine spawn/exec primitive — the `MeshCommand` union (`src/core/MeshRpc.ts` lines 28–105) contains no `remote-spawn`/`remote-exec`/`enroll-on-peer` verb (the only cross-machine command-execution is `remote-close`, a relayed session kill, not a login spawn). So "operator on machine A taps 'enroll account X on VM B' → A makes B run its own `EnrollmentWizard`" has no mechanism today; R6's "minted ON the target, never transits" guarantee is currently about a code path that isn't there. The build MUST pick ONE in writing:
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:186:**R9 — Mechanism A is a separate, provider-gated, opt-in flag; BOTH mechanisms require a trusted target.** Sealed-credential transport is NOT reached by `multiMachine.accountFollowMe` alone. It requires a distinct `multiMachine.accountFollowMe.credentialTransport` **per-provider allowlist** (default EMPTY — never a global boolean, so a future operator cannot flip Anthropic into a ToS violation by accident), AND the mandate's `mechanism: 'credential-transport'`. It is REFUSED for any provider whose ToS prohibits token movement — **Anthropic is excluded from the default allowlist** per `SubscriptionPool.ts` lines 17–21. For a refused provider, the system falls back to Mechanism B with an honest operator notice ("this provider requires a per-machine login; I'll walk you through it"). No dead end (P8). Mechanism A also REFUSES a target machine that is not an established, trust-gated mesh peer with a verified `encryptionPublicKey` (the bootstrap-paradox guard: a fresh cloud VM has no key to seal to until it is paired+trusted — never auto-trust a new VM to receive a credential). **The SAME established-trusted-peer floor applies to Mechanism B (S7):** because Mechanism B mints and holds a real, refreshable live credential on the target, authorizing it onto a machine IS trusting that machine with a live credential — a target may NOT clear a lower bar just because "nothing crossed the wire." Both mechanisms gate the target at the same trust floor.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:197:**R13 — Seal-time status revalidation is the load-bearing guard; detection is necessary but insufficient (gap-confirmed retarget).** The original wiring to `PeerVisibilityGuard.detectImproperRevocations` (`PeerVisibilityGuard.ts` lines 49–67) was mis-targeted: that helper is a PURE hygiene check that flags entries which ARE revoked but are MISSING `revokedBy`/`revokeReason` audit fields — it does NOT detect a peer that still treats a de-paired machine as ACTIVE (a peer whose registry never received the revocation), which is the `peer-missing`/`peer-flapping` surface, a separate code path. A peer with a stale registry showing machine X `active` would pass `detectImproperRevocations` cleanly and STILL seal a credential to the de-paired X — the exact hole. **Therefore the load-bearing guard is at SEAL TIME:** the credential-seal path (`provisionAccountTo`) MUST re-validate the destination's CURRENT status from authoritative registry state (status === `'active'` AND not revoked AND key/epoch-current per R4b) — NEVER from a possibly-stale cached peer view — before sealing. **Safe-direction default: if the seal-time status is unknown or stale beyond a freshness bound, REFUSE the seal (fail-closed), do not seal optimistically.** `detectImproperRevocations` remains wired as a complementary monitoring layer (it catches non-propagated/under-audited revocations after the fact), but it is NOT the guarantee — refusing-to-seal-to-a-machine-whose-revocation-this-machine-hasn't-heard-of is. (Guards S3, S4; gap 8.)
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:221:1. On the target, `EnrollmentWizard.start()` runs (the wizard's own `driveLogin` executes ON the target VM, R6), with `configHome` set to a fresh per-account slot. The wizard already isolates each login to its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, handles the two-code Claude flow (`flowNotice`, lines 106–115) — subject to R6b's full-two-code poll-window contract — and auto-reissues on expiry (lines 153–179). A remote drive throw surfaces an honest "couldn't start the login on `<nickname>` — retry?" (R6b), never a silent stuck pending-login. Follow-me adds nothing secret to the wizard.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:235:**6.1 `SubscriptionPool` (registry follow-me = metadata only).** A redacted `SubscriptionAccount` projection replicates over the WS2 store as a new `subscription-account-meta` JournalKind whose receive-side validator + namespace + consistency model are defined normatively in **§6.1a** (do NOT ship the new kind without the matching `validateData` branch — see the code-verified consequence below). The outbound projector RE-RUNS `assertNoCredentialFields()` (R2 defense-in-depth) and STRIPS `configHome`. The recipient stores the meta in a foreign namespace its local reads UNION (WS2 union-reader discipline), so `GET /subscription-pool?scope=pool` (WS5.1) shows a peer's account depth/quota without holding its login. **First-hop sender binding (inherited, named here as a relied-upon guarantee):** `JournalSyncApplier.apply` derives the replica file from `senderMachineId` only, so a peer physically cannot write into another peer's namespace — `?scope=pool` reads attribute the OWNING machine for account A from the namespace-derived sender, NEVER from an owning-machine field inside the payload (there is no such field in the whitelist). **PII honesty:** the projection includes `email` — operator/provider PII that lands at-rest in each same-operator peer's plaintext coherence-journal replica and audit lines. This is accepted as low-stakes (email is "NEVER a secret" per `SubscriptionPool.ts` lines 89–93; exposure is bounded to registered same-operator peers); `configHome` and all credential fields are stripped. Named here rather than left silent.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:244:**6.2 `AnthropicSubscriptionRouter` (routing never silently crosses a credential boundary).** The router (`AnthropicSubscriptionRouter.evaluate`) selects an Anthropic path for an *internal* call on *this* machine; it MUST NOT route a call to a peer's account. Add a `locallyExecutable` gate to account selection: an account is executable on machine M iff M's `SubscriptionPool` holds it with a real local `configHome` AND a valid login (status active/warming, not needs-reauth). A meta-only account (replicated-in from a peer, no local config-home) is `locallyExecutable: false` and is invisible to `evaluate()`'s fallback/`force` paths — closing the `force`-mode "use an account I have metadata for but no credential" hole at *selection* time, not at call time.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:252:**6.5 `SecretSync` vs the credential verb — tightly fenced, OAuth-excluded, distinct path.** Mechanism A does NOT ride the existing `secret-share` verb/`SecretShareHandler`/`decryptFromSync` (permissive RBAC, no AAD — R3a, §3.1); it uses the DISTINCT `account-credential-share` verb + `decryptAccountCredential`. The existing `SecretSync` machinery (per-recipient X25519, receive-only-by-default via `pushEnabled`) contributes its *posture* — receive-only default, per-recipient encryption, registered-peer-only, decrypt-fails-closed — which the new credential path inherits and TIGHTENS (operator-mandate-gated RBAC, seal-time status revalidation, AAD fail-closed). For Anthropic the config-home is the credential location and never serializes into a `Secrets` value — so there is literally nothing to encrypt, and the boundary is enforced by **absence of a serializer**, not a runtime check that could regress. **Explicit rules:** (1) the build round MUST NOT add a "push the config-home" or "push the OAuth token" path to the legacy `secret-share`/SecretSync flow; (2) a lint/test MUST assert no `configHome`/OAuth field is ever marked `{secret:true}` (so it can never ride `provisionAll`'s broadcast); (3) a Tier-2 test asserts `SecretShareHandler.handle` rejects (or never receives) an `account-credential-share` payload — the legacy decryptor is unreachable for credential-class data.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:260:- *Clock skew / stale-replay gaming the meta merge* → the REAL guarantee (downgraded from the prior draft's mis-cited "skew-suspicious quarantine," which does NOT exist in code — CODE-VERIFIED: `JournalSyncApplier`'s only quarantine path is incarnation-flip-driven, `handleIncarnationFlip`, and there is NO HLC-skew quarantine): account-status/quota for account A is authoritative ONLY from A's holder stream (first-hop sender binding namespaces by `senderMachineId`; a non-holder peer structurally cannot assert A's status), so the merge is **last-writer-wins on the single holder stream**, guarded against a same-peer old-seq replay by the existing **strict per-(peer,kind) monotonic seq** (`validateEntry`: `seq <= lastHeldSeq` → silent duplicate drop, line 506; `seq !== lastHeldSeq+1` → `invalid`, line 507) **plus incarnation fencing**. This blocks a replayed-stale or rolled-back `subscription-account-meta` from resurrecting a dead account's "healthy" status on the holder stream (the soft availability bug); it is NOT a cross-peer HLC-skew defense (none is needed, because cross-peer assertion of A's status is structurally impossible). The §6.1a consistency model is the normative statement; WS2's HLC contributes ORDERING within a stream, not a skew-quarantine.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:264:- *Stale-active peer seals to a de-paired machine* → R13 seal-time status revalidation refuses the seal (fail-closed on unknown/stale status); detection is the complementary monitoring layer, not the guarantee.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:274:- **Per-VM independent quota is a FEATURE** — with Mechanism B each followed account has its OWN login → its OWN quota window as the provider sees it; across N VMs this is the desired multi-account behavior the pool already exploits (auto-swap, depth tie-breaker, WS5.1). The spec notes this explicitly so no one "optimizes" toward a single shared credential (Mechanism A) thinking it cleaner — it is worse for BOTH quota distribution AND blast radius.
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:315:- **Key-rotation durability across reboot + anchor-rollback resistance (S3, R4b):** the rotated recipient X25519 key / generation survives a VM reboot (durable in the OS-keychain / encrypted-`SecretStore` anchor, not in-memory or a plaintext JSON file) so a reboot cannot resurrect the old key and replay an old sealed blob; AND, after rotation, reverting the on-disk anchor to its pre-rotation state STILL leaves an old captured sealed blob undecryptable (proves the anchor is rollback-resistant per R4b's invariant, not merely reboot-surviving).
docs/specs/ws52-account-follow-me-security.md:352:> **Note (was OQ3 — pairing-epoch anchoring, now SETTLED in R4b):** the anchoring decision is no longer an open question — R4b fixes the anchor as OS-keychain-where-available / AES-256-GCM-`SecretStore`-fallback (never a bare JSON file) with the rollback-resistance invariant, because PR1 must ship a working AAD check against `pairingEpoch`. Resolved per the operator's "design-fork decisions are mine" standing directive.
