docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:15:1. **major** — `scripts/lint-enforcement-fingerprint.mjs` is still defeated by a heading collision, and the certification written for the fix is false. Appending a NEW article whose `###` heading duplicates an already-fingerprinted article, with **no** fingerprint of its own, admits a new standard with no enforcement fingerprint and both new guards print clean. Reproduced in a clone at `…/scratchpad/attack`: `lint-enforcement-fingerprint: clean — 89 article(s), 6 fingerprinted, 82 grandfathered` (exit 0) and `lint-enforcement-gap-records: clean` (exit 0). 6 + 82 = 88, not 89 — the number that exposes the hole is again computed, printed, and compared to nothing, directly falsifying the guard's own comment (`scripts/lint-enforcement-fingerprint.mjs:150-152`, and the same sentence in `docs/STANDARDS-REGISTRY.md:682`): *"after the refusal the printed count and the verified set are the same number by construction."* The gap record's matched verdict (`docs/enforcement-gaps.json`, `GAP-name-keyed-population-collision`) also claims each guard now refuses "on its own evidence rather than on a sibling's" — for this half of the collision space the only refusal comes from the sibling `scripts/lint-no-duplicate-definitions.mjs`, which the author explicitly disclaimed borrowing. The record's evidence string calls this case "non-fingerprinted duplicate correctly silent here"; for the fingerprint-requirement lint that silence is the guard being defeated, not correctness.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:17:2. **major** — Comment exclusion is still bypassable inside a **handled** form. `withoutComments()` (`scripts/lint-deferral-referent-resolves.mjs:175`) strips `//` only when not preceded by `:` (`/(^|[^:])\/\/[^\n]*/g`). Proven by execution in the clone: `docs/specs/zzz-probe.md` carrying `<!-- tracked: ZZZ-90007 -->` plus `src/zzz-probe.ts` containing `export const o = { a:// ZZZ-90007\n 1 };` → `{"trackedIds":218,"resolved":17,"orphans":201,"failures":[]}` — a brand-new marker resolved through pure commentary, and the lint reported clean. TypeScript carries 240 of the live resolutions by the guard's own header, so this is the dominant arm. Pass 9's finding was "comments beginning after punctuation"; the shell instance was fixed, the same instance in the largest corpus was not. "Prose and comments do not resolve a referent" remains false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:19:3. **major** — The invisible-payload regression test still does not regression-test the route. Proven by execution: deleting the `if (hasNoVisibleCharacters(text)) { … }` block from `src/server/routes.ts:14925` in the clone leaves `tests/unit/telegram-reply-invisible-payload.test.ts` green (4 passed). Only two files reference the predicate (`grep` over all `.ts/.js/.mjs/.cjs`), and the test never constructs the app. The repo already ships the stronger remedy pass 9 named first — `tests/unit/localhost-link-guard-route.test.ts` is a ~60-line `createRoutes(ctx)` + express harness testing a deterministic guard on this exact route — and it was not copied. The file's own header calls itself a "Regression test for the invisible-payload refusal on POST /telegram/reply/:topicId"; it is not one.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:25:6. **major** — The three historical ratchets still constrain nothing here. `git cat-file -e origin/main:<path>` returns NO for all of `docs/enforcement-fingerprint-baseline.json`, `docs/deferral-referent-baseline.json`, `docs/enforcement-gaps-floor.json`, so every comparison takes the `establishing` path with `*_BASE_REQUIRED=0`. The internal hash chain (`scripts/lib/baseline-history.mjs`) has no external anchor, so wholesale rewrite-and-rechain validates. Carried unchanged from passes 7 and 9; not converted to dated named work.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:41:- **(e) PARTIAL.** Attack executed in a temp clone. *Attack A* (two articles under one heading, **both** fingerprinted) is refused by both guards, each naming the collision — the demonstrated shape is genuinely closed. *Attack B* (duplicate heading, new article carries **no** fingerprint) passes both guards clean and admits an unfingerprinted new standard (finding 1). Full chain: I extracted the 45 `node` steps from `package.json`'s `lint` and ran every one against attack B — `lint-no-duplicate-definitions.mjs` fails (`DUPLICATE DEFINITION …:895 — article "Iterative Audit to Convergence" is already defined at line 655`) and `generate-standards-hierarchy.mjs --check` fails. So the chain differs from the two guards individually: the exploit does not ship green, but the coverage is borrowed from a sibling the fix explicitly claimed not to borrow from. I searched for an asymmetry that would evade the sibling too (blockquote unwrapping, fence handling, CR handling, indented headings) and found none — the sibling is strictly broader.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:43:- **(g) CLOSED.** `GAP-name-keyed-population-collision` exists, passes the three-leg schema, and is swept: `lint-enforcement-gap-records: clean — 6 gap(s), 6 swept against the live population of 6 fingerprinted standard(s)`; the sweep partitions 2 matched + 4 unmatched. It is also the first record with `hadNoFingerprint: false` (the other five still use the exception).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:57:Not declining on either series. Pass 9's single dip did not hold. More decisive than the level: each of the last four passes has found a **new** load-bearing defect introduced by the immediately preceding repair — pass 7 found the `--update-baseline` writer erasing the chain it was protecting; pass 8 found the invisible-payload guard covering one code point instead of the class; pass 9 found the regression test detached from production; pass 10 finds the collision refusal covering one half of the collision space while certifying it covers the whole. The repair rate and the defect-introduction rate inside the repairs are matched.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:65:1. **Two consecutive passes at ≤1 load-bearing defect**, where the one remaining is outside the certification machinery itself. One clean-ish pass after nine is a sample of one; the series has already produced a 5→3 dip that reverted.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:66:2. **Zero new load-bearing defects introduced by the repairs of the previous pass.** This is the actual blocker: the stream is flat *because* fixing is generating. Until one full pass finds nothing new inside the last pass's repairs, the level cannot fall.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:72:**No.** Seven specific mismatches between the repository's account of itself and its machinery, all verified by execution: (i) `STANDARDS-REGISTRY.md:682` and `lint-enforcement-fingerprint.mjs:150-152` say the printed count and the verified set are now the same by construction — attack B prints 89/6/82; (ii) the same passage says both guards refuse on their own evidence — for half the collision space only the sibling refuses; (iii) the deferral guard says prose and comments do not resolve a referent — a `.ts` comment after a colon resolves a new marker; (iv) `tests/unit/telegram-reply-invisible-payload.test.ts` calls itself a route regression test — deleting the route's guard leaves it green; (v) `GAP-no-moment-declared.outsidePopulation` says 82 and 86 for the same quantity in adjacent fields; (vi) `STANDARDS-REGISTRY.md:170` says 93% and 92% for the same measurement in one paragraph; (vii) the repository's own required coverage gate rejects the submitted state. The registry is markedly more candid than at pass 1 — the falsification notes, the residual sizing, the borrowed-coverage concession are all real and unusual — but candour about past over-claims is not the same as the current account being true, and the current account is not.
docs/specs/ws23-relationships-userregistry-security.eli16.md:82:you approve, nothing replicates relationship/user PII.
docs/specs/slack-test-workspace-principal-source.eli16.md:3:> The one-line version: give the Slack demo cast a safe home OUTSIDE the real user list, so a routine cleanup of that list can't secretly break who's-allowed-to-do-what — and lock that home to one specific test workspace so those pretend roles can never leak into a real one.
docs/specs/slack-test-workspace-principal-source.eli16.md:11:- **The permission gate** — decides allow / refuse / clarify / step-up for each Slack request. Working, in observe-only (logs decisions, blocks nothing). Not the thing being changed.
docs/specs/slack-test-workspace-principal-source.eli16.md:22:- **It only answers for one specific workspace.** The block names the test workspace's id, and the source only resolves the cast while the adapter's *verified* connection (what Slack itself reported at startup) matches that id. In any other workspace the block is completely invisible — not a fallback, not a merge, just gone.
docs/specs/slack-test-workspace-principal-source.eli16.md:33:**Prevents pretend roles leaking into a real workspace.** The source is locked to the verified connected team id from Slack's own startup handshake — config alone is never trusted. Point the adapter at a different workspace and the cast is structurally invisible; production behavior is byte-for-byte identical to having no cast at all.
docs/specs/slack-test-workspace-principal-source.eli16.md:43:One PR, dark by default. Nothing changes for any agent unless a Slack config explicitly adds the `testCast` block with `testWorkspace: true` — which only the live-test workspace ever will. Enforcement (`enforce: true`) remains a later, separately-gated step that still needs a clean observe round first.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:11:1. **DESIGN — Alternative Telegram parameter encodings bypass the guard.** The door checks only non-empty string bodies, while every other `BodyInit` and every query-string request falls through directly to `fetch`: [telegram-egress.ts:61](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:61), [telegram-egress.ts:63](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:63), [telegram-egress.ts:78](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:78). Telegram supports URL-query, form-encoded, JSON, and multipart parameters, so the comment that body-carrying methods necessarily use JSON is false: [telegram-egress.ts:54](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:54), [Telegram Bot API request rules](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#making-requests). Failure path: a reader-visible method sent through any supported non-string encoding reaches the network without `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:13:2. **DESIGN — URL and method recognition are textual, not equivalent to Telegram dispatch.** `BOT_API_URL` is case-sensitive and returns the method spelling unchanged: [telegram-egress.ts:27](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:27), [telegram-egress.ts:38](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:38). The field map is then indexed case-sensitively and an absent entry silently returns: [invisible-payload.ts:125](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:125), [invisible-payload.ts:311](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:311). Telegram method names are case-insensitive, and URL hostnames are case-insensitive. Failure path: an equivalent host or method spelling is dispatched by Telegram while the door derives `null` or an unmapped method and forwards it unchecked.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:15:3. **DESIGN — Moving the boundary deleted closed-world method enforcement.** The source still declares guarded and bodyless sets, but `telegramFetch` never consults the bodyless set or rejects an unknown method; `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible` simply returns when the map has no field: [invisible-payload.ts:142](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:142), [invisible-payload.ts:168](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:168), [invisible-payload.ts:312](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:312). The replacement lint checks only network confinement: [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:92](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:92). Failure path: a newly used reader-visible Bot API method passes through the approved door, receives no content decision, and leaves while lint remains clean. This is the remaining “guard that nothing guards.”
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:17:4. **DESIGN — The boundary lint still requires call-resolution knowledge it claims to have eliminated.** It recognizes only a call whose callee syntax is the bare identifier `fetch`: [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:98](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:98). A property-access call such as the platform’s global fetch, or another non-bare network-fetch binding, is invisible even with a literal Bot API URL. Conversely, a locally shadowed function named `fetch` is treated as network egress. Failure path: Telegram network access outside the door uses an ordinary non-bare fetch reference; no violation is recorded, contradicting the “exactly one file” proof at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:19](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:19).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:19:5. **DESIGN — The retained HTML/Markdown transforms are still parser approximations and still over-refuse.** The HTML regex removes every tag-shaped substring without establishing that Telegram recognizes valid markup: [invisible-payload.ts:274](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:274), [invisible-payload.ts:292](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:292). The “undecidable” escape applies only when extraction is completely empty: [invisible-payload.ts:319](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:319), [invisible-payload.ts:335](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:335). Failure path: malformed or unsupported tag-shaped visible source plus a remaining invisible text node produces a non-empty invisible extraction and terminal refusal at [TelegramAdapter.ts:1435](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:1435), although Telegram’s parse rejection would have activated the plain fallback that displays the source. Thus “true without qualification” and “provably receives nothing” are false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:21:6. **PRECISION — The consolidation left the source and specification describing the deleted architecture.** `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible` is still called a deliberately second check beside a pre-format check: [invisible-payload.ts:303](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:303). The same file still says both funnels call the pre-format guard first and that the deleted predecessor lint enforces it: [invisible-payload.ts:156](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:156), [invisible-payload.ts:184](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:184). Adapter and Lifeline comments likewise refer to a nonexistent pre-format call: [TelegramAdapter.ts:5762](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:5762), [TelegramLifeline.ts:2916](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2916). The spec declares CMT-1246 shipped at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:196](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:196), then later describes it as still-open and blocking at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:376](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:376). Failure path: a maintainer trusts nonexistent independent guard and closed-world lint layers while changing the sole remaining enforcement point.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:23:7. **PRECISION — The all-marks repair remains false in the governing spec, fixture inventory, and route diagnostic.** Runtime includes `M`: [invisible-payload.ts:64](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:64), and tests deliberately accept lone `Mn`, `Mc`, and `Me` marks: [telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:297](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:297). The spec still defines content as only L/N/P/S and says a lone mark does not pass: [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:225](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:225), [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:235](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:235). Its claimed fixture inventory—eight non-printing and five positive controls—is actually seven and ten: [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:452](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:452), [telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:282](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:282). The route also reports every refusal as whitespace/zero-width despite rejecting controls, unassigned, private-use, and blank glyphs: [routes.ts:14938](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/server/routes.ts:14938). Failure path: operators and future reviewers receive a false reason and an obsolete description of the predicate and its cross-engine drift surface.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:31:1. Still open as described: the sole surviving `assertTelegramPayloadVisible` sender call is the tokenless relay guard at [TelegramAdapter.ts:1401](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:1401), before the cross-machine handoff.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:33:2. Still open as described: the predicate uses runtime Unicode property escapes at [invisible-payload.ts:64](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:64) and [invisible-payload.ts:76](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:76); no vendored table exists. The spec’s narrower “L/N/P/S-only, fail-safe” account is incorrect as covered by finding 7.
