docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:4:The pass-47 discriminator table is faithful to `Client.cpp`, and the two priority-union repairs are present. The guarantee still fails in the two other native `InputRichMessage` arms: `markdown` and `html` are reduced with the old scalar regexes rather than Telegram's rich grammar, and a nonempty media declaration is treated as rendered without establishing that the winning source references it. One further table entry makes a false semantic claim about dividers. Thus both the runtime guarantee and the requirement that every supporting claim be true fail.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:13:   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:584-599` turns the entire winning `markdown` or `html` source into a scalar leaf. That leaf goes through `readerVisibleText` at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:352-360`, whose Markdown model removes only ordinary inline links and whose HTML model removes only tags. The result is admitted on the first category-positive source character at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:653-657`. Telegram, however, natively parses inline and block formulas in both rich Markdown and rich HTML, and specifies that formula bodies are raw LaTeX source ([Rich Message Formatting Options](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#rich-message-formatting-options)). The pass-47 reasoning at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:490-493` correctly says source characters are not rendered glyphs, but only `type: "mathematical_expression"` reaches that OPAQUE branch (`src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:416-417,506-512`). The regression at `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:498-518` likewise exercises only the explicit-block arm; `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:324-329` gives the HTML arm only a styled zero-width literal, not rich grammar.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:19:   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:589` defines `mediaPresent` as any nonempty array; `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:591-599` copies that boolean to `undecidable`; and `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:655` unconditionally allows it before examining the leaves. The comment at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:581-582` says presence renders. The API defines the array instead as media **specified in** the `markdown` or `html` source using an id-bearing `tg://...` link ([InputRichMessage](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#inputrichmessage)). The Bot API parser likewise passes source and media as separate inputs (`Client.cpp:11633-11640` in the [official server](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/master/telegram-bot-api/Client.cpp)); TDLib retains the source and media table separately (`RichMessage.cpp:98-117`) and derives received rich-message media from the parsed blocks (`RichMessage.cpp:75-79`) in the [official implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tdlib/td/master/td/telegram/RichMessage.cpp). Presence is a declaration, not proof of a rendered reference.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:21:   Concrete failure path: the winning `markdown` or `html` source contains only mechanically invisible rendered text, while `media` contains a syntactically valid entry whose id is never referenced by that source. The guard returns solely because the array is nonempty. The server parses the winning source without a media block, so the unused definition contributes nothing to the reader; the remaining text also renders as nothing. This recreates pass 47's discarded-member-vouching defect one layer above the discriminator table.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:23:3. **PRECISION — the table says a divider renders nothing, but a divider is itself a rendered horizontal rule.**
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:25:   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:453-454` places both `divider` and `anchor` in `BLOCK_RENDERS_NOTHING` and comments that both render nothing. Telegram defines `InputRichBlockDivider` as a divider corresponding to `<hr/>` ([Bot API, InputRichBlockDivider](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#inputrichblockdivider)); only the anchor is a non-rendering jump target. `blockScan` therefore returns the `NOTHING` state at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:535` for a block that visibly renders.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:27:   Concrete failure path: an explicit-block rich message containing a divider enters `blockScan`, is classified as proved-empty, then is allowed by the no-leaf return at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:656`; the reader sees a rule. The runtime decision happens to be safe for the one-way guarantee, but the table comment and the meaning assigned to `NOTHING` are false. This is load-bearing precision: closing the stated-open no-leaf allowance by trusting this state would refuse real rendered content, and the current table cannot support the claimed `NOTHING`/OPAQUE distinction.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:34:- **`answerCallbackQuery` and `editForumTopic` — CONFIRMED AS PRODUCT JUDGMENTS, not new.** Their opposite classifications remain at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:121-123,159-160,186-208`; neither is derived mechanically.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:37:- **Structured content in query/form/multipart — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** Those encodings remain scalar strings at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:207-269`; only object-valued fields enter `structuredFieldScan` at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:653-654`. The query regression at `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:409-415` even calls a serialized rich structure “checked” although it remains a string.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass48-verdict.md:39:- **No-leaf structures are allowed — CONFIRMED OPEN AND NOT CARRIED FAR ENOUGH, not new.** `NOTHING` and `OPAQUE` are distinct scan values at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:388-400`, but enforcement permits both at lines 655-656. A proved-non-rendering anchor is therefore still allowed, and opaque content is also an unconditional waiver. Finding 3 separately records the new fact that `divider` was put in the wrong side of that distinction.
